# Drawing a plot: print.ggplot

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`print.ggplot()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/print.ggplot.html), which covers
`plot.ggplot()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and
then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

A ggplot2 plot is drawn when it is printed, and at R's top level that happens by itself: an
expression whose value is a plot draws it. Inside a function or a `for` loop nothing is printed
by itself, so a plot made there is drawn only by calling `print()` or `plot()` on it, which
returns the plot invisibly. A Giggly plot is a value, and building it draws nothing:
`Giggly.to_svg/2` draws it, returning an SVG document, which in Livebook `Kino.Image` shows below
the cell.

## Plots made in a loop

```r
colours <- c("class", "drv", "fl")

# Doesn't seem to do anything!
for (colour in colours) {
  ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy, colour = .data[[colour]])) +
    geom_point()
}
```

Each pass of the loop makes a plot and discards it, so R draws nothing. A Giggly plot is never
drawn by itself either: only `Giggly.to_svg/2` draws it.

## Printing plots in a loop

```r
for (colour in colours) {
  print(ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy, colour = .data[[colour]])) +
          geom_point())
}
```

`print()` draws each plot in turn: `hwy` against `displ`, its points coloured by `class`, then by
`drv`, then by `fl`. Not in Giggly yet: mapping `colour`, the scale that maps it and the legend
that reads it back.
